        \textbf{Abstract:} A large body of survey evidence shows that firms' subjective macroeconomic expectations diverge from realised conditions and shape pricing and hiring decisions but whether this expectations channel is empirically distinguishable from the effect of realised shocks and whether either of the channel is strong enough to change individual labour mobility, remains largely unexplored in the Indian context. In this paper, I address the following research question: \textbf{Do inflationary expectations have an effect on labour mobility and industry switching?} I use the Centre for Monitoring Indian Economy's (CMIE) Industry Outlook for macroeconomic series, which is wave and industry matched with CMIE's Consumer Pyramids Household Survey (CPHS) which tracks same individuals across successive four-monthly waves, and the Business Inflation Expectations Survey (BIES) fielded by the Misra Centre for Financial Markets and Economy at Indian Institute of Management Ahmedabad which surveys firms' backward looking cost assessments and forward looking cost and inflation expectations. These results suggest that for unidirectional industry-switching margin, labour reallocation among Indian workers tracks realised cost shocks more than firms' own expectations of future costs and inflation, a finding which qualifies rather than confirms the more recent macro-expectations literature.
        
        \vspace{0.2cm}

