\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
Firms' cost and inflation expectations could plausibly affect workers through atleast four distinct margins: (i) \emph{wage} margin --- the pay increments or renegotiated compensation a worker receives while being in the same job; (ii) \emph{migration} margin --- the worker has to shift from one place to another with no possibility of role switching in the same company in the new place; (iii) \emph{promotion/demotion} margin --- whether a worker's occupational tier or status changes without a change in employer; and (iv) \emph{job-retention and reallocation} margin --- whether a worker keeps, looses, or switches out of their current job, employer, or industry altogether. This paper studies a specific part of margin (iv): whether a worker's reported 2-digit NIC industry of employment changes between consecutive time period blocks due to a set of given factors (Macroeconomic variables and BIES variables). 

This paper is a correlation analysis, that addresses whether the expectations channel can be empirically distinguishable from the effect of realised macroeconomic conditions and can either channel be strong enough to move individual labour mobility. While there have been experimental analysis in different part of the world (Coibion et al., 2018 \cite{coibion2018firms}; Candia et al., 2023 \cite{candia2023macroeconomic}), but this paper could be a start to this area of research in the Indian context. This paper mainly asks: \emph{do inflationary expectations have an effect on labour mobility and industry switching?} 

I answer this question by combining three rich, high-frequency Indian data sources. The first is the Consumer Pyramids Household Survey (CPHS), a household panel administered by the Centre for Monitoring Indian Economy (CMIE), an individual-level household panel that re-interviews the same respondents roughly every four months (called "Waves"). Each wave records employment status, occupation (NCO), industry (NIC), employment type (formal/informal/casual/self-employed), and a battery of demographic and financial-inclusion variables. The second is CMIE's Industry Outlook database, which supplies wave-matched macroeconomic time series - CPI inflation, WPI input-cost inflation, industry-matched Index of Industrial Production (IIP) growth, and industry-matched bank credit growth - merged onto each CPHS respondent by their reported broad industry of employment (\texttt{BROAD\_INDUSTRY\_NAME}) and survey wave. 

The third is the Business Inflation Expectations Survey (BIES), fielded by the Misra Centre at IIM Ahmedabad, which surveys about 8000 manufacturing firms on current cost conditions (Q1--Q3), forward-looking cost expectations (Q4), and CPI inflation expectations (Q5) every month and consolidates the response percentages into expectation groups; BIES responses are aggregated to wave-level weighted means (and, for Q4, a wave-level standard deviation capturing expectation dispersion) and merged onto the CPHS panel by wave. BIES coverage corresponds to CPHS waves 25--39 (approximately January 2022 to April 2026). Merging these data sources, based on NIC 2-digit codes, allows me to test whether individual-level labour reallocation - specifically, whether a worker switches industry or occupational tier between consecutive waves - responds to (a) realised macroeconomic conditions (CPI inflation, WPI input-cost inflation, industry-matched industrial output growth, and industry-matched credit growth), and (b) firms' own elicited expectations of costs and inflation, over and above what is captured by realised conditions alone. This paper addresses only cross-industry reallocation margin of labour mobility and not as a comprehensive measure of every way a worker's family can respond to a shock.

