The Main Sample (Section \ref{sec:results}) consists of all CPHS respondents who are earning members observed in at least two consecutive waves — the minimum required both for the individual fixed-effects estimator and for the industry-switch outcome to be defined. This yields 1,751,291 person-wave observations for the demographic controls; restricting further to observations with non-missing lagged macro regressors leaves 416,343 (Table \ref{tab:table1_summary}, column (1)). 

The BIES-Manufacturing Sample (Sections \ref{subsec:robustness_check_1}–\ref{subsec:robustness_check_2}) further restricts the Main Sample to (i) waves with BIES coverage and (ii) manufacturing workers, defined as 2-digit NIC divisions 10–33. Applying the same two-sample logic as above, the BIES-Manufacturing Sample yields 43,137 person-wave observations with non-missing lagged CMIE macro proxies, of which 38,473 also have a non-missing industry-switch outcome (Table \ref{tab:table1_summary}, column (2)).

Table \ref{tab:table1_summary} reports summary statistics for the two samples side by side. On average, 34.9 \% of Main-Sample person-wave pairs involve an industry switch, compared with a higher 39.2 \% in the BIES-Manufacturing Sample — consistent with greater job-to-job churn among manufacturing workers relative to the broader CPHS population. The BIES-Manufacturing subsample also faces somewhat higher mean WPI input-cost inflation (113.7 vs. 52.5 in Main-Sample index units) and lower mean credit growth (8.9 \% vs. 13.2 \%), reflecting both its shorter (BIES-covered) time window and its manufacturing-specific composition. Demographic and financial-inclusion controls (age, education, marital status, bank account, PF coverage, health insurance, mobile access) are summarized only for the Main Sample in Table \ref{tab:table1_summary}; in the BIES-Manufacturing regressions of Table \ref{tab:table4a_bies_vs_cmie} these enter as unreported controls rather than headline variables of interest.

