The empirical strategy proceeds in two complementary tracks. The main-sample track uses the full CPHS panel merged with CMIE Industry Outlook macroeconomic series, estimating individual and industry fixed-effects linear probability models (LPMs) of industry switching on one-wave-lagged macro variables, following an adaptive-expectations logic in which lagged realised values proxy for the macroeconomic conditions that agents could plausibly have anticipated. The robustness-check track restricts the sample to CPHS manufacturing workers (NIC divisions 10--33) observed in waves with BIES coverage, and directly compares the explanatory power of lagged CMIE macro proxies against lagged BIES-elicited firm expectations for the same outcome and the same workers, allowing a head-to-head test of whether \emph{directly elicited} expectations carry independent information beyond realised conditions. Akarsu et al. (2025) \cite{akarsu2025inflation}, discussed further in \ref{subsec:expectations}, provide a complementary, experimentally-identified estimate of how an induced revision in a firm's own expectations moves the same firm's pricing and hiring in Turkey; the exercise here asks a related but distinctive question with representative, non-experimental data: do firms' \emph{equilibrium} expectations, as they naturally arise are elicited in BIES, leave a detectable footprint on realised worker mobilility, over and above realised macro conditions, in a labour market where informal and causal employment - rather than formal hiring and firing is the dominant margin of adjustment?

The Main Sample results, however, provide only a limited evidence that realised macro conditions predict industry switching once aggregate wave effects are absorbed, and a leads-and-lags placebo test further shows that current switching is also predicted by \emph{future} realised macro conditions - pattern consistent with clean causal, backward looking reading of the lagged coefficients. The sharper test comparing lagged CMIE macro proxies against lagged BIES-elicited firm expectations on the identical manufacturing subsample and wave set shows that realised WPI input-cost inflation significantly predicts industry switching, while none of the BIES expectations do despite a larger estimation sample, and this result is the same for the placebo test as well. Taken together, the evidence suggests that, for the industry-switching margin studied here, Indian workers' reallocation tracks \emph{realised} cost shocks more closely than it tracks firms' own \emph{elicited} expectations of future costs and inflation. This qualifies, rather than confirms, the presumption in much of the macro-expectations literature that subjective expectations are a first order driver of real labour-market decisions, and points instead to backward-looking, liquidity and survival driven adjustment as the dominant force behind labour reallocation in a labour market where informal and casual employment absorbs most of the shock. This matters for how policymakers and firms should think about the transmission of inflation surprises to Indian labour markets like interventions or communication strategies aimed at managing firms' or workers' \emph{expectations} of future costs may have limited traction to actual reallocation behaviour relative to interventions that address the \emph{realised} cost and liquidity conditions driving it.

\section{Literature Review}
\label{sec:literature_review}
This paper's central contribution is an observational, representative-sample test of whether Indian workers' industry-switching behaviour tracks realised macroeconomic shocks or firms' directly elicited expectations of future costs and inflation, exploiting a novel merge of a firm expectation survey (BIES) onto a large household labour panel (CPHS). This research draws on, and contributes to, three strands of literature: (i) the literature on how firms and households form macroeconomic expectations and how those expectations feed into real decisions; (ii) the macro-labour literature on how aggregate shocks and labour-market institutions jointly determine employment and reallocation outcomes; and (iii) the emerging literature on informal labour markets and job-loss dynamics in India.

\subsection{Firm and household macroeconomic expectations}
\label{subsec:expectations}
A large body of work has documented that the expectations that firms and households report in surveys deviate systematically from full-information rational expectations, and that these deviations have real consequences. Coibion et al. (2018) \cite{coibion2018firms} provide foundational evidence, that firm expectations about inflation and other macro aggregates are poorly anchored, exhibit substantial disagreement, and rational inattention. Candia et al. (2023) \cite{candia2023macroeconomic} synthesise this literature in expectations correlating with firms' pricing, investment, and hiring decisions.

Most closely related to the present paper is Akarsu et al. (2025) \cite{akarsu2025inflation}, who use a randomised control trial embedded in a firm survey in Turkey to show that experimentally induced revision in a firm's inflation expectations causally moves from the same firm's own subsequent pricing and hiring decisions. My setting does not have experimental variation in expectations; instead, I exploit the panel structure of a firm expectations survey (BIES) merged with a household labour-market panel (CPHS) to ask a complementary, non-causal but nonetheless informative question: do the correlations between labour reallocation and macro variables look different, in sign or magnitude, when the labour-market-relevant ``expectations'' regressor is a firm's own elicited probabilistic forecast rather than an aggregate statistic compiled by a government or research agency? A null result on the BIES regressors is therefore not evidence against Akarsu et al.'s (2025) \cite{akarsu2025inflation} causal finding; at most it shows that whatever local causal effect elicited expectations have on an individual firm's own decisions, that effect is not detectable as an aggregate, wave-level predictor of realised work reallocation in the Indian data used here - a plausible reason for this divergence is elaborated in Section \ref{subsec:informality}.

Gautier et al. (2025) \cite{gautier2025firms} study firms' inflation and wage expectations show that firms revised their wage-setting expectations upward in response to elevated inflation expectations, with heterogeneous pass-through across sectors depending on labour-market tightness. Their finding that wage and price expectations co-move, and that this co-movement varies by sector-level labour-market conditions, motivates my choice to interact macro shocks with sectoral and occupational classifications (NIC and NCO) in the labour-reallocation regressions below, and to conduct separate heterogeneity analysis by gender and rural/urban location, given well-documented differences in labour-market tightness and bargaining power across these groups in the Indian context.

\subsection{Macroeconomic shocks, labour-market institutions, and Reallocation}
\label{subsec:shocks}
The classical macro-labour literature emphasises that the employment response to macroeconomic shocks depends jointly on the shock itself and on labour-market institutions. Chen et al. (2003) \cite{chen2003labour} develop a dynamic labour-demand model in which the effect of firing costs on employment is expected to fall, higher firing costs primarily discourage hiring rather than deterring layoffs, so that institutions that are protective in good times can be harmful to employment exactly when protection seems most needed. This motivates both the lagged/adaptive-expectations specifications (which allow effects to build up over one to two waves) and the heterogeneity analysis by region and gender.

Goux et al. (2001) \cite{goux2001fixed} study the dynamics of labour demand under fixed-term contracts using French establishment-level panel data and GMM estimators (Hansen, 1982 \cite{hansen1982large}), showing that the elasticity of labour demand to output and cost shocks differs sharply between fixed-term and permanent contract margins. Their fixed-effects and lag-based identification strategy parallels the individual fixed-effects approach, and underscores the importance of distinguishing margins of adjustment in a setting - like India's - where formal job protection covers only a minority of workers.

\subsection{Informality and labour-market risk in India}
\label{subsec:informality}
Kumari et al. (2025) \cite{kumari2025coping} study how Indian workers cope with labour-market risk following formal job loss, documenting substantial and persistent earnings losses and a marked shift toward informal employment following displacement from formal jobs. Their informalisation finding that adverse shocks push workers from formal into informal, casual, or lower-tier work rather than simply raising unemployment - is the central reason to expect the Indian expectations channel might look different from Turkey's: with roughly four-fifths of Indian workers informally employed and largely outside the Industrial Disputes Act's job-protection regime, the margin of adjustment available to Indian firms facing a cost or demand shock is not primarily the formal hire/fire margin, but movement of workers across formal, informal, casual, and self-employed tiers. If reallocation for the median Indian worker is driven by contemporaneous liquidity and survival constraints rather than forward-looking job search, realised shocks should dominate elicited expectations in predicting industry switching even if firms' expectations-formation process is qualitatively similar to that documented in Turkey - the pattern the results below in fact show. This also motivates the financial-inclusion controls (bank account access, provident fund coverage, health insurance, mobile access) included throughout as buffers against distress-driven reallocation.

\section{Methodology and Empirical Strategy}
\label{sec:empirical_strategy}
Taken together, this literature motivates two testable implications for industry-switching margin: (i) macro shocks operating through firm cost and margin channels (WPI input-cost inflation) and through household purchasing power (CPI inflation) should predict industry switching, with the CPI effect's sign left ambiguous a priori since it can trigger both voluntary search and forced churn; and (ii) if firms' own directly elicited expectations carry information beyond realised conditions, BIES regressors should retain explanatory power net of the CMIE proxies in the manufacturing sub-sample.

The empirical analysis in this paper tests two parallel sets of hypotheses, both stated in terms of the single outcome variable actually estimated throughout the paper: \texttt{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH}, a binary indicator for whether a worker's 2-digit NIC industry changes between consecutive waves. \textbf{Set A} tests realised macroeconomic conditions (the transmission channel actually operating in the economy); \textbf{Set B} tests firms' directly elicited (BIES) expectations of costs and inflation (the channel implied by the research question itself). Comparing the two sets tests whether industry switching responds to \emph{actual} conditions, \emph{anticipated} conditions, or both.

Because \texttt{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH} does not distinguish whether a switch moves a worker up or down the occupational or formality ladder, every hypothesis below is stated only in terms of the \emph{probability of switching}, not its direction. Directional hypotheses about upward versus downward occupational mobility - which would require the \texttt{UPWARD\_MOB}/\texttt{DOWNWARD\_MOB} outcomes defined in the Appendix (Table \ref{tab:vardef}) - are a natural extension of this framework but are not estimated in this paper, and so are deliberately omitted here rather than posed and left untested.

\subsection{Baseline specification: Realised macro conditions (Set A)}
The baseline specification estimates a linear probability model (LPM) of industry switching on one-wave-lagged realised macroeconomic conditions, following the adaptive-expectations logic described in the Appendix (Section \ref{subsec:estimation_notes}): since firm- and worker-level expectations are not observed at the individual-wave frequency for the full CPHS sample, lagged realised macro values proxy for the macroeconomic conditions that agents could plausibly have anticipated going into the current wave. The one-way fixed-effects specification is
 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:oneway}
\begin{gathered}
    \text{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH}_{i,t} = \beta_1 \, \text{L\_INF\_CPI}_{i,t-1} + \beta_2 \, \text{L\_WPI\_INPUT}_{i,t-1} + \beta_3 \, \text{L\_IIP\_INDUSTRY}_{i,t-1} + \\
    \beta_4 \, \text{L\_CREDIT\_INDUSTRY}_{i,t-1} + X_{i,t}'\gamma + \delta_{j(i)} + \delta_{j(i)} \times \text{WAVE\_NO} + \alpha_i + \varepsilon_{i,t}
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
 
\noindent where $i$ indexes individuals, $t$ indexes survey waves, $j(i)$ denotes individual $i$'s broad industry of employment, $\alpha_i$ is an individual fixed effect (absorbing all time-invariant individual heterogeneity), $\delta_{j(i)}$ is a broad-industry fixed effect, $\delta_{j(i)} \times \text{WAVE\_NO}$ is an industry-specific linear wave trend, and $X_{i,t}$ is the vector of individual controls (age, age-squared, education, marital status, bank-account access). The two-way fixed-effects specification augments Equation \eqref{eq:oneway} with wave fixed effects $\lambda_t$:
 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:twoway}
\begin{gathered}
    \text{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH}_{i,t} = \beta_1 \, \text{L\_INF\_CPI}_{i,t-1} + \beta_2 \, \text{L\_WPI\_INPUT}_{i,t-1}  + \beta_3 \, \text{L\_IIP\_INDUSTRY}_{i,t-1} + \\ 
    \beta_4 \, \text{L\_CREDIT\_INDUSTRY}_{i,t-1} + X_{i,t}'\gamma + \delta_{j(i)} + \delta_{j(i)} \times \text{WAVE\_NO} + \lambda_t + \alpha_i + \varepsilon_{i,t}
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
 
\noindent Wave fixed effects absorb any aggregate, economy-wide shock common to all individuals in a given wave, so identification of $\beta_1$--$\beta_4$ in Equation \eqref{eq:twoway} comes purely from \emph{cross-industry} variation in macro conditions within a wave, net of the industry-specific linear trend. Both specifications are estimated by the within (fixed-effects) estimator, with standard errors clustered at the individual level (\texttt{vce(cluster MEM\_PANEL\_ID)}) throughout, and coefficients on the (unreported) industry fixed effects and industry-wave trends are absorbed but not tabulated.
 
\subsection{BIES robustness specification: direct expectations (Set B)}
To test whether firms' \emph{directly elicited} expectations carry independent explanatory power beyond realised conditions, I re-estimate the industry-switching model on the BIES-Manufacturing Sample, replacing the CMIE macro proxies with one-wave-lagged BIES survey aggregates:
 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:bies}
\text{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH}_{i,t} = \sum_{k} \beta_k \, \text{BIES\_LAG}_{k,t-1} + X_{i,t}'\gamma + \lambda_t + \alpha_i + \varepsilon_{i,t}
\end{equation}
 
\noindent where $\text{BIES\_LAG}_{k,t-1} \in \{\text{L1\_q1\_net\_balance}_{t-1}, \text{L1\_q2\_net\_balance}_{t-1}, \text{L1\_q3\_wtd\_mean}_{t-1}, \\
\text{L1\_q4\_wtd\_mean}_{t-1}, \text{L1\_q5\_wtd\_mean}_{t-1}\}$ are the lagged BIES profit-margin balance, sales-level balance, backward-looking cost change, forward-looking cost expectation, and CPI inflation expectation, respectively. Because BIES regressors vary only at the wave level (not at the individual or industry level), broad-industry fixed effects and industry-wave trends are omitted from Equation \eqref{eq:bies} to avoid collinearity with the wave fixed effects $\lambda_t$; individual fixed effects $\alpha_i$ are retained. For direct comparability, I estimate an analogous specification using the CMIE macro proxies (\texttt{L\_CREDIT\_INDUSTRY}, \texttt{L\_IIP\_INDUSTRY}, \texttt{L\_WPI\_INPUT}, \texttt{L\_INF\_CPI}) on the \emph{same} BIES-Manufacturing Sample and wave set, so that any difference in explanatory power between the CMIE-proxy and BIES-direct columns of Table \ref{tab:table4a_bies_vs_cmie} reflects the information content of the regressor rather than a difference in sample composition. If the realised-conditions (CMIE) coefficients are significant while the BIES-direct coefficients are not, this indicates that industry switching in the manufacturing subsample responds to realised macro conditions rather than to firms' own elicited expectations.

\subsection{Leads-and-lags Specification: A test for pre-trends and reverse causality}
\label{subsec:leadlag}
A lagged coefficient being significant in Equation \eqref{eq:twoway} is consistent with a causal, backward-looking channel running from realised macro conditions to industry switching, but it is equally consistent with reverse causality (industry switching itself shifting the industry-matched macro series) or with a pre-existing trend that happens to be correlated with the lag. A standard way to probe this is to add the one-wave-\emph{led} value of each macro regressor alongside its lag: if switching behaviour today is not actually anticipating tomorrow's macro conditions, the led terms should be jointly indistinguishable from zero, which supports a causal reading of the lagged coefficients. I estimate,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:leadlag}
\begin{gathered}
    \text{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH}_{i,t} = \sum_{k} \beta_k \, X_{k,i,t-1} + \sum_{k} \phi_k \, X_{k,i,t+1} + X_{i,t}'\gamma + \delta_{j(i)} + \delta_{j(i)} \times \text{WAVE\_NO} \\
    + \lambda_t + \alpha_i + \varepsilon_{i,t}
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $X_{k,i,t-1} \in\ {\text{L\_INF\_CPI}, \text{L\_WPI\_INPUT}, \text{L\_CREDIT\_INDUSTRY}\}}$ are the same one-wave lags as in Equation \eqref{eq:twoway}, and $X_{k,i,t-1}$ are the corresponding one-wave-leads. The $\beta_k$ retain their causal interpretation from Section \ref{subsec:baseline_spec}; the $\phi_k$ are the placebo coefficients with no causal meaning of their own and their role is more diagnostic. I estimate the Equation \eqref{eq:leadlag} on the main sample (Table \ref{tab:table3b_leadlag} and for direct comparability with the BIES-Manufacturing subsample for robustness checks (Table \ref{tab:table4b_leadlag}), in both cases with individual and wave fixed effects and standard errors clustered at the individual level.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}
 
In the one-way specification (Table \ref{tab:table2_oneway_indswitch}), industry-matched IIP growth is positively and significantly associated with industry switching ($\hat{\beta}=0.033$, $p<0.01$): a one-point increase in lagged industry output growth is associated with a 3.3 percentage-point increase in the probability of switching industry in the current wave. This is consistent with the idea that expanding industries generate more job-to-job movement (both into and possibly out of the industry, since \texttt{INDUSTRY\_SWITCH} does not distinguish direction), rather than with the Set A downward-mobility hypothesis specifically, which predicts a \emph{negative} relationship between IIP growth and downward mobility - a hypothesis that requires the directional (\texttt{UPWARD\_MOB}/\texttt{DOWNWARD\_MOB}) outcomes rather than the undirected switch indicator used here. The coefficients on lagged CPI inflation, WPI input-cost inflation, and industry credit growth are small in magnitude and not statistically distinguishable from zero in the one-way specification.

\input{Tables_TEX/Table2_OneWay_FE_IndustrySwitch}

Once wave fixed effects are added (Table \ref{tab:table3a_twoway_indswitch}), the IIP coefficient falls from 0.033 to 0.026 and loses statistical significance, while the point estimates on the other three regressors change little. This attenuation is expected: much of the wave-to-wave variation in IIP growth is common across industries (i.e.\ it reflects the aggregate business cycle documented in Figure \ref{fig:trends_facets}), and once this common component is absorbed by $\lambda_t$, the remaining cross-industry variation used to identify $\beta_3$ in Equation \eqref{eq:twoway} is considerably noisier. Overall, the Main Sample results provide only weak evidence that realised macro conditions predict undirected industry switching once aggregate wave effects are controlled for, which motivates the more targeted BIES-manufacturing comparison in Sections \ref{subsec:robustness_check_1}--\ref{subsec:robustness_check_2}.

\input{Tables_TEX/Table3a_TwoWay_FE_IndustrySwitch}

Table \ref{tab:table3b_leadlag} reports the leads-and-lags test of Equation \eqref{eq:leadlag} on the main sample. The lagged terms remain broadly consistent with the two-way FE results above: lagged CPI inflation is now positively significant (0.027), lagged WPI input-cost inflation and lagged IIP growth are negatively significant (-0.06 and -0.016 respectively) once the leads are included and the sample is reconstituted around the lead-lag window. More importantly for identification, all four \emph{led} terms are statistically significant --- F.CPI, F.WPI, F.IIP, and F.Credit. Because these placebo terms should be indistinguishable from zero under a purely backward-looking, causal reading of the lagged coefficients, this is evidence against a clean causal interpretation in the Main Sample: current industry switching behaviour is not independent of \emph{future} realised macro conditions, consistent with either reverse causality, anticipatory switching ahead of macro conditions that were in fact to some extent forceable, or a pre-existing trend correlated with both. This qualifies the Main Sample findings in Sections \ref{sec:results} as descriptive robust correlations rather than a causal-identified adaptive-expectations channel.

