\section{Robustness Check}

\subsection{BIES Direct Expectations}
\label{subsec:robustness_check_1}
The Main Sample results in Section \ref{sec:results} use lagged \emph{realised} macro conditions as adaptive-expectations proxies, since individual-level expectations are not observed for the full CPHS panel. BIES, however, directly elicits firm-level cost and inflation expectations for a manufacturing subsample of CPHS respondents, allowing a sharper test of whether \emph{directly elicited} expectations matter for labour reallocation. This section restricts the sample to the BIES-Manufacturing Sample defined in Section \ref{sec:data} - CPHS manufacturing workers (NIC 10--33) in waves with BIES coverage, observed in at least two consecutive waves - and reports summary statistics before turning to the direct CMIE-versus-BIES model comparison in Section \ref{subsec:robustness_check_2}.

Table \ref{tab:table1_summary} shows that, on average, firms in the BIES-Manufacturing Sample report profit margins below normal (\texttt{L1\_q1\_net\_balance} mean $=-0.412$) and sales levels below normal (\texttt{L1\_q2\_net\_balance} mean $=-0.404$) relative to their own stated baseline, alongside a mean backward-looking cost-change assessment (\texttt{L1\_q3\_wtd\_mean}) of 5.2\%, a mean forward-looking cost expectation of 4.8\%, and a mean CPI inflation expectation of 4.7\% - both expectation measures somewhat below the realised cost-change assessment, consistent with firms expecting some moderation in cost pressure going forward. The BIES-Manufacturing industry-switch rate (39.2\%) and lagged CMIE macro proxy means broadly mirror the Main Sample (Table \ref{tab:table1_summary}), restricted to manufacturing waves with BIES coverage.

\subsection{BIES VS.\ CMIE Direct Comparisons}
\label{subsec:robustness_check_2}
Table \ref{tab:table4a_bies_vs_cmie} implements the head-to-head comparison of Equation \eqref{eq:bies} against the analogous CMIE-proxy specification, both estimated on the identical BIES-Manufacturing Sample and wave set with individual and wave fixed effects.

Column (1) uses the lagged CMIE macro proxies. Lagged WPI input-cost inflation is negatively and significantly associated with industry switching ($\hat{\beta}=-0.001$, $p<0.05$), while lagged CPI inflation, industry-matched IIP growth, and industry-matched credit growth are not statistically significant, though the IIP coefficient is comparatively large in magnitude (0.152). Column (2) replaces these with the lagged BIES-elicited expectations: none of the five BIES regressors - the profit-margin balance, sales-level balance, backward-looking cost assessment, forward-looking cost expectation, or CPI inflation expectation - is statistically distinguishable from zero, despite a substantially larger sample (30,064 vs.\ 7,104 observations, reflecting greater non-missingness of the wave-level BIES aggregates relative to the industry-matched CMIE series). The two specifications also differ sharply in fit: the CMIE-proxy model explains considerably more within-individual variation (within $R^2=0.066$) than the BIES-direct model (within $R^2=0.005$).

Because a Set A (realised-conditions) coefficient is significant in column (1) while none of the Set B (BIES-direct) coefficients are significant in column (2), the evidence in this manufacturing subsample favours the conclusion that industry-switching behaviour responds to \emph{realised} cost conditions - specifically wholesale input-cost inflation - rather than to firms' own directly elicited cost and inflation expectations. This runs counter to the framing implicit in the paper's research question, which anticipated an independent expectations channel; instead, the results suggest that, at least for the undirected industry-switching margin studied here, backward-looking realised shocks dominate forward-looking subjective expectations as a predictor of labour reallocation. 

