\input{Tables_TEX/Table4b_LeadsLags_BIES_IndustrySwitch}

Section \ref{subsec:leadlag} flagged that the Main Sample lagged coefficients fail a leads-and-lags pre-trend test, so I repeat that test here on the CMIE macro proxies within the BIES-Manufacturing subsample and wave set, to see whether the WPI result of column (1) above survives the same scrutiny.

Table \ref{tab:table4b_leadlag} shows a markedly cleaner pattern than the Main Sample counterpart (Table \ref{tab:table3b_leadlag}). None of the four lagged terms is statistically significant in the smaller sample, and of the four led terms, only F.IIP is weakly significant ($\hat{\phi}=-0.043$, $p<0.10$); F.CPI, F.WPI, and F.Credit are all statistically indistinguishable from zero. Because the placebo (led) terms are largely null here, this leads-and-lags test does not turn up the same reverse causality or pre-trend concern that it does in the Main Sample, which leads more credibility to a causal reading of the realised WPI input-cost result reported in column (1) of Table \ref{tab:table4a_bies_vs_cmie}, though the loss of significance on the WPI \emph{lag} itself once leads are added, together with the smaller cell sizes underlying this subsample, means this should be seen as a supportive causal confirmation rather than definitive.

These finding should be interpreted with the caveats that (i) the BIES-Manufacturing Sample is substantially smaller and more volatile than the Main Sample, and (ii) BIES regressors vary only at the wave level and so are identified off considerably less variation than the industry-matched CMIE series.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper asked whether inflationary expectations affect labour mobility and industry switching among Indian workers, using a merged CMIE CPHS--BIES panel spanning waves 25--39 (January 2022--April 2026). Using individual and industry (or wave) fixed-effects linear probability models of industry switching on lagged realised macro conditions, the Main Sample results (Section \ref{sec:results}) show only limited evidence that realised macro conditions predict industry switching once aggregate wave effects are absorbed: industry-matched IIP growth is significantly positive in a one-way specification but loses significance once wave fixed effects are added. Restricting to a manufacturing subsample with BIES coverage and directly comparing lagged CMIE macro proxies against lagged BIES-elicited firm expectations (Sections \ref{subsec:robustness_check_1}--\ref{subsec:robustness_check_2}) sharpens this picture: lagged WPI input-cost inflation significantly predicts industry switching, while none of the five BIES expectation measures do, despite a larger estimation sample. Taken together, these results suggest that, for the undirected industry-switching margin examined here, labour reallocation among Indian workers tracks \emph{realised} cost shocks more closely than it tracks firms' own \emph{elicited} expectations of future costs and inflation - a finding that qualifies, rather than confirms, the expectations-channel hypothesis motivating this paper. A leads-and-lags placebo test (Section \ref{subsec:leadlag}) qualifies this further, in the Main Sample, where current switching is predicted not only by lagged macro conditions but also by their one-wave-\emph{led} values. Therefore, the Main Sample lag coefficients are to be seens as robust descriptive correlations rather than causally identified effects. The same placebo test on the BIES-Manufacturing subsample, where the led terms are largely insignificant giving somewhat more support to a causal reading of the realised WPI input-cost result that anchors the paper's central claim.

Returning to the question raised in Section \ref{subsec:expectations}: what does this exercise add beyond Akarsu et al. (2025) \cite{akarsu2025inflation}'s causal finding that experimentally induced expectations revisions move Turkish firms' pricing and hiring? The contribution is not causal test of the same mechanism in a new country, since the BIES-CPHS design here has no exogenous source of variation in expectations. Rather, the finding that realised conditions (WPI input-cost inflation, and more weakly, industry output growth) predict worker mobility while wave aggregates elicited expectations do not, which is consistent with the structural story developed in \ref{subsec:expectations}: that in a labour market where dominant adjustment margin is movement across formal, informal, and casual employment tiers rather than formal hiring and firing, workers and firms alike may reallocate in response to shocks that have already materialised rather than shocks they anticipate. An equally plausible alternative reading, which the present design cannot rule out, is purely methodological: BIES expectations are observed only at the wave level and merged onto workers by industry rather than by firm, so any true firm-level expectations effect of the kind Akarsu et al. identify could be attenuated to statistical insignificance by this aggregation and mismatch, independent of whether the underlying mechanism is weaker in India. Adjudicating between these two explanations - a genuinely weaker expectations channel versus an aggregation artefact - requires either a firm-to-worker matched BIES-CPHS linkage or a source of plausibly exogenous variation in Indian firms' expectations (for instance, differential regional or sectoral exposure to a common information shock, in the spirit of Akarsu et al.'s randomised design); both are natural next steps and are noted among the limitations below.
 
Several limitations point to directions for further work. First, the outcome variable used throughout is the undirected industry-switch indicator. Second, the BIES-Manufacturing Sample is small relative to the Main Sample and BIES regressors are identified only off wave-level variation, which limits statistical power relative to the industry-matched CMIE proxies; a longer BIES panel or firm-level (rather than wave-aggregated) BIES linkage would sharpen this comparison. Third, the heterogeneity analysis by gender and rural/urban location motivated by Gautier et al. (2025) \cite{gautier2025firms} and Chen et al. (2003) \cite{chen2003labour} in Section \ref{sec:literature_review}, and the financial-inclusion buffer variables (bank account, provident fund, health insurance, mobile access) motivated by Kumari et al. (2025) \cite{kumari2025coping}, are constructed in the Appendix variable set (Table \ref{tab:vardef}) but not yet incorporated into the reported specifications. Addressing these three extensions is left for the next stage of this research.

