\subsection{Estimation notes}
\label{subsec:estimation_notes}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Operationalisation of macroeconomic expectations:} Since firm- and worker-level expectations survey data are not available at the required individual-wave frequency for the full CPHS sample, macroeconomic expectations are operationalised throughout using the adaptive-expectations framework of Equation (1). The one-wave (L1) and two-wave (L2) lags of each macro regressor serve as empirical proxies for the macroeconomic expectations.
    \item \textbf{Fixed Effects Setup:} All FE models absorb \texttt{MEM\_PANEL\_ID} as the individual fixed effect and include wave fixed effects via \texttt{i.WAVE\_NO} where applicable. The within-estimator differences out all time-invariant individual characteristics.
    \item \textbf{Standard Errors:} Standard errors are clustered at the individual level (\texttt{vce(cluster \\
    MEM\_PANEL\_ID)}) in all specifications across all three analysis tracks.
    \item \textbf{BIES Wave-level Regressors:} BIES regressors vary only at the wave level. Wave fixed effects are therefore omitted from BIES-track regressions (Sections 6-7) to avoid perfect collinearity.
    \item \textbf{BIES Coverage Period:} The BIES coverage period corresponds to CPHS waves 25-39 (approximately January 2022 -- April 2026).
    \item \textbf{Mobility Sample Constraints:} NIC- and NCO-interaction-based mobility outcomes are defined by the interaction equations of Section 3.2. The combined mobility tables (Tables 14-15) restrict the sample to \texttt{EARNING\_MEMBER} $=1$ and require at least two consecutive panel observations per individual.
    \item \textbf{Statistical significance:} * $p<0.10$, ** $p<0.05$, *** $p<0.01$.
\end{itemize}

